{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $z=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-8265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =2(0+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-8265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78277b67c5ea3cc29d1be4bffc13aa6605e41ff3e33d0eae06dbef12541c60e5"} {"question" "q-8265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-8265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=2+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "z=2\\text{i}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "z=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "z=\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-8265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=2\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $z=\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{4}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-2\\text{i})}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{8}{5}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{80}{25}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ is positive and $y=-\\frac{8}{5}$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2 \\\\  & ≈5.176,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176)).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "78277b67c5ea3cc29d1be4bffc13aa6605e41ff3e33d0eae06dbef12541c60e5"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176))"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.381)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.381))"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.914)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.914))"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3.107)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3.107))"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4.381)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4.381))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-69966" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.176)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.176))"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-107564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $z=10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da5290772662786589f59487e0c5d1265941382a9e43f43d1d5106323af10025"} {"question" "q-107564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =10(0+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =10\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994893/state.json" "file_sha256" "da52907726